\begin_layout Standard
If you are bypassing the Pico SDK entirely to write a raw,
 bare-metal register driver for the Pico 2 W (RP2350) to communicate with the CYW43439,
 you face a significant constraint:
 Infineon does not publish the low-level register map for the CYW43439.
 [1,
 2] 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
However,
 because the chip uses a standard Broadcom/Cypress gSPI (generic SPI) Backplane architecture,
 the open-source community has successfully reverse-engineered the low-level bit-banging sequences.
 [1,
 3] 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
To write to GPIO 0 completely bare-metal,
 your RP2350 firmware must manually orchestrate the following three layers:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
------------------------------
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
## Phase 1:
 Set up the RP2350-to-CYW43439 Physical Bus
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The physical connection between the RP2350 and the CYW43439 on the Pico 2 W board uses a unique shared 3-pin SPI bus (Clock,
 MOSI,
 MISO share a line or rely on high-speed half-duplex turnarounds) plus a Chip Select line.
 [4] 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
* 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
* SDIO_CLK:
 RP2350 GPIO 29
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
* SDIO_CMD (MOSI/MISO):
 RP2350 GPIO 24
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
* SDIO_DATA_0 (MISO/IRQ):
 RP2350 GPIO 25
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
* WIFI_CS_N:
 RP2350 GPIO 23
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
* 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
You must configure these pins using the RP2350’s native Pad Control and SIO registers,
 or implement a low-level state machine using the RP2350's PIO (Programmable I/O) blocks to handle data directional shifting dynamically.
 [5] 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
------------------------------
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
## Phase 2:
 Execute a gSPI Register Write Protocol
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The CYW43439 splits its addressable internal memory into separate logical functions.
 To write data,
 your RP2350 must stream a precise 32-bit Command Word followed by your payload over SPI:
 [5,
 6] 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
$$
\backslash
text{Command Word Bits:} 
\backslash
quad [
\backslash
text{Write/Read (1 bit)}] 
\backslash
quad [
\backslash
text{Auto-Increment (1 bit)}] 
\backslash
quad [
\backslash
text{Function Number (2 bits)}] 
\backslash
quad [
\backslash
text{Register Address (15 bits)}] 
\backslash
quad [
\backslash
text{Data Length (13 bits)}]$$ 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
1.
 Pull CS Low (GPIO 23).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
2.
 Transmit the 32-bit Command Word header.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
3.
 Transmit the Data payload (usually 1 to 4 bytes for discrete register toggles).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
4.
 Pull CS High.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
------------------------------
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
## Phase 3:
 The Bare-Metal Register Sequence for GPIO 0
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
To turn on the LED connected to GPIO 0,
 your code must perform a sequence of direct writes to specific internal registers:
 [7] 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
## 1.
 Wake the Chip Core
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Before the chip responds to peripheral register updates,
 you must configure the gSPI Status/Control layer (Function 0).
 [6] 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
* 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
* Write to the SpiCtrl register (0x0000 via Function 0) to configure the interface mode (e.g.,
 word length,
 endianness).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
* Write 0x02 to the WakeUp register (0x0002 via Function 0) to clear sleep states and enable the backplane clock.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
* 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
## 2.
 Set GPIO 0 as an Output
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Next,
 route into Function 1 (Backplane/System Interconnect) to reach the core configuration registers.
 [7] 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
* 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
* Target the GPIO Controller Base Register Address (typically offset 0x18002000 in the Broadcom core).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
* Set Direction:
 Write a bitmask to the GPIODir register (Offset 0x08) where Bit 0 = 1 (setting GPIO 0 as an output).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
* 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
## 3.
 Write the Pin State (gpioout)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
To update the pin state,
 you write a composite 32-bit packet containing a Mask and a Value to the GPIOOut register (Offset 0x04):
 [7] 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
* 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
* To Turn LED ON (High):
 Set Mask to 0x0001 and Value to 0x0001.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
* To Turn LED OFF (Low):
 Set Mask to 0x0001 and Value to 0x0000.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
* 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
------------------------------
\end_layout

